    title: "The Full SEO Audit",
    prompt: `When to use it: Any time you want to know exactly why your website isn't ranking, what's hurting your visibility in both Google and AI search systems like ChatGPT and Perplexity, and what to fix first — without paying an agency thousands of dollars to tell you things you could have found yourself. Paste your codebase or website details and get a production-grade audit covering everything from metadata and site structure to how AI systems read and cite your content. You are an elite technical SEO strategist, AI search optimization specialist, and senior full stack engineer. Your task is to fully audit, architect, and implement both traditional SEO and AI-era search optimization across this entire codebase and product experience. Do not give shallow recommendations. Do not give generic SEO advice. Do not only output a checklist. You must deeply inspect the actual architecture, routing, rendering strategy, metadata systems, content structure, performance patterns, APIs, schema markup, semantic structure, internal linking systems, AI readability, and discoverability patterns inside this project. Treat this like a production-grade growth and discoverability optimization initiative for a serious internet product. Your objectives: 1. Maximize discoverability in traditional search engines 2. Maximize visibility in AI search systems and LLM-generated answers 3. Improve crawlability and indexing quality 4. Improve semantic understanding of the product and pages 5. Improve authority signaling 6. Improve structured data quality 7. Improve page speed and rendering performance 8. Improve machine readability for AI systems 9. Improve citation likelihood in AI-generated responses 10. Improve long term scalable SEO architecture SECTION 1 — FULL SEO AUDIT Perform a deep audit of: metadata architecture, title generation, meta descriptions, canonical handling, robots directives, sitemap generation, Open Graph tags, Twitter cards, structured data/schema markup, semantic HTML usage, heading hierarchy, accessibility impact on SEO, crawlability, internal linking, duplicate content risks, URL structures, redirect handling, rendering strategy, hydration issues, JavaScript SEO risks, lazy loading problems, image optimization, alt text quality, mobile optimization, Core Web Vitals, caching strategy, internationalization SEO issues, pagination/indexation issues, thin content risks, auth wall discoverability problems, client-side rendering risks, orphan pages, and index bloat risks. Identify what is implemented correctly, what is missing, what is actively harmful, what is limiting rankings/discoverability, and what prevents AI systems from understanding the product. Be extremely specific. Reference actual files, components, routes, rendering flows, and architecture decisions from the project. Explain WHY each issue matters. SECTION 2 — AI SEO / LLM OPTIMIZATION AUDIT Analyze this project specifically for discoverability and usability inside ChatGPT retrieval systems, AI answer engines, Perplexity-style systems, Gemini-style systems, AI summaries, retrieval augmented generation systems, semantic indexing systems, and future AI-native discovery systems. Audit for: semantic clarity, entity clarity, contextual ambiguity, machine-readable explanations, content chunking quality, knowledge extraction friendliness, structured meaning density, authoritativeness signals, source attribution likelihood, AI citation likelihood, FAQ structure quality, definitional clarity, topical coverage depth, brand/entity reinforcement, relationship mapping between concepts, contextual reinforcement, terminology consistency, hidden knowledge trapped in UI-only experiences, lack of crawlable explanatory content, weak informational architecture, poor semantic labeling, and weak machine comprehension. Identify what would make AI systems misunderstand this product, what prevents AI systems from citing this site, what reduces retrievability in LLM systems, what content should exist but does not, what semantic structures should be added, what explainer systems should exist, and what AI-readable content layers should be created. SECTION 3 — COMPETITOR-LEVEL SEO ARCHITECTURE IMPROVEMENTS Design a scalable SEO architecture strategy. Include: programmatic SEO opportunities, semantic content hub opportunities, landing page strategies, entity-based SEO structures, topical authority systems, glossary systems, FAQ architecture, internal linking architecture, scalable schema systems, AI-readable knowledge architecture, dynamic metadata systems, structured content systems, indexing prioritization strategies, content clustering, semantic routing structures, and authority reinforcement systems. Think beyond blog spam. Think like a search strategist building a category-defining product. SECTION 4 — IMPLEMENTATION PLAN Create a prioritized implementation roadmap across five priorities: critical technical fixes, structural SEO improvements, AI SEO enhancements, authority and scaling systems, and advanced future-proofing improvements. For every recommendation include the exact issue, why it matters, implementation approach, affected files/components/routes, technical difficulty, SEO impact level, AI SEO impact level, and expected outcome. SECTION 5 — DIRECT CODE IMPLEMENTATION Do not stop at recommendations. Actually implement the improvements directly into the codebase where appropriate. This includes metadata improvements, structured data systems, schema markup, sitemap systems, robots configuration, semantic HTML improvements, accessibility improvements impacting SEO, internal linking improvements, Open Graph improvements, performance improvements, rendering optimizations, crawlability improvements, AI-readable content structures, FAQ schema systems, entity reinforcement systems, and semantic improvements. Make safe, production-grade edits only. Do not break existing functionality. Preserve design quality and UX integrity. SECTION 6 — AI SEARCH DOMINANCE STRATEGY Design strategies specifically for increasing probability of being cited by LLMs, appearing in AI-generated summaries, becoming a trusted retrievable source, improving semantic authority, improving knowledge graph association, improving entity recognition, and improving machine confidence in this brand/product. Include structured explanation systems, AI-readable documentation layers, semantic reinforcement patterns, citation-friendly formatting, entity consistency strategies, expert signal systems, trust and authority indicators, machine-readable educational content, and ontology-style relationships if relevant. SECTION 7 — FINAL OUTPUT Return: executive summary, critical SEO risks, critical AI SEO risks, highest leverage improvements, full implementation report, files modified, remaining recommendations, long term growth opportunities, estimated impact of changes, and what separates this project from competitors after optimization. Important instructions: Think like both a search engineer and a machine learning retrieval engineer. Do not output surface-level SEO advice. Do not assume SSR automatically solves SEO. Inspect actual implementation details. Prefer semantic clarity over keyword stuffing. Prioritize machine comprehension as much as rankings. Optimize for humans, crawlers, and AI systems simultaneously. Treat AI discoverability as equally important as Google rankings. Favor scalable systems over one-off optimizations. Explain tradeoffs where relevant. If the architecture itself limits SEO, say so directly. Be brutally honest where needed. Do a real implementation-grade audit and optimization pass worthy of a high-growth production company competing for long term discoverability in both search engines and AI-native discovery systems.`,
